From 9db4114527a0250ca241f8fdc154d247e09a9f1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ant=C3=B3nio=20Sarmento?= <Keyaku@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 15:15:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] SurfaceFlinger: re-arm the primary display's HWC vsync after
 a hotplug

setPrimaryVsyncEnabledInternal() stores mHWCVsyncPendingState but only pushes it
to HWC while the internal display exists and is powered on, and nothing
re-applies it afterwards except an OFF -> ON power transition. On the GPD XD+ an
external hotplug (the mini-HDMI mirror) can leave the composer with vsync
generation stopped while SF still believes it is enabled: SF then dispatches no
vsync, composes nothing, and both displays freeze with the hardware still
scanning out. Measured in that state: "mLastDispatchTime: 43368 ms ago", the
composer's VSyncThread_0 parked in futex instead of waiting on kernel vsync, and
only a screen off/on cycle recovering it.

Since HWComposer::setVsyncEnabled() early-returns when the request matches its
cached value, a plain re-request cannot resynchronize a composer that stopped
silently, so force DISABLE then restore the pending state.

Necessary but NOT sufficient on its own: the vendor composer clears display 1's
internal connected flag asynchronously, after hotplug processing has finished, so
this re-arm can fire before vsync is actually dropped. A timer-driven watchdog is
the planned follow-up; this closes the "pending state is never re-applied" hole
regardless.

Changes:
- Force a DISABLE -> pending-state transition on the internal display's HWC vsync at the end of processDisplayHotplugEventsLocked()

Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I2cb7cc5ae830744597f5556fdbac63c758d08c16
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index 878761b077..dcb2b27117 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -2498,6 +2498,33 @@ void SurfaceFlinger::processDisplayHotplugEventsLocked() {
     }
 
     mPendingHotplugEvents.clear();
+
+    // GPD XD+ (§102): re-arm the primary display's HWC vsync after any hotplug.
+    //
+    // setPrimaryVsyncEnabledInternal() stores mHWCVsyncPendingState but only
+    // pushes it to HWC when the internal display exists and is powered on, and
+    // nothing re-applies it afterwards except an OFF -> ON power transition. An
+    // external hotplug (the mini-HDMI mirror coming or going) can leave the
+    // composer with vsync generation stopped while SF still believes it is
+    // enabled: SF then dispatches no vsync at all, composes nothing, and BOTH
+    // displays freeze with the hardware still scanning out. Measured in that
+    // state: "mLastDispatchTime: 43368 ms ago", the composer's VSyncThread_0
+    // parked in futex rather than waiting on kernel vsync, and only an
+    // off/on screen cycle recovering it.
+    //
+    // HWComposer::setVsyncEnabled() early-returns when the requested state
+    // matches its cached value, so a plain re-request cannot resynchronize a
+    // composer that has silently stopped. Force the transition instead: drop to
+    // DISABLE and immediately restore the pending state, which makes HWC issue
+    // both calls for real.
+    if (const auto displayId = getInternalDisplayIdLocked()) {
+        const sp<DisplayDevice> display = getDefaultDisplayDeviceLocked();
+        if (display && display->isPoweredOn() &&
+            mHWCVsyncPendingState == hal::Vsync::ENABLE) {
+            getHwComposer().setVsyncEnabled(*displayId, hal::Vsync::DISABLE);
+            getHwComposer().setVsyncEnabled(*displayId, hal::Vsync::ENABLE);
+        }
+    }
 }
 
 void SurfaceFlinger::dispatchDisplayHotplugEvent(PhysicalDisplayId displayId, bool connected) {
